    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "University Park grew around New Mexico State University, evolving from agricultural roots in the Mesilla Valley into a student‑oriented enclave.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/university_park_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The area began as ranchland south of Las Cruces, then a 119.43‑acre site was donated by Jacob and Bertha Schanblin in 1889 for the college.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/university_park_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1918, the platting of residential lots formalized University Park, highlighted by the Prairie‑style Nason House built for the university president.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/university_park_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The arrival of the Atchison, Topeka and Santa Fe Railroad in Las Cruces in 1881 spurred regional growth that enabled the university’s expansion into surrounding lands.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/university_park_new_mexico",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "University Park’s community thrives on the university’s presence, offering students access to libraries, sports facilities, and research centers while keeping a suburban feel.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/university_park_new_mexico",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The neighborhood’s name reflects its origin as a planned residential enclave adjacent to the land‑grant institution that became New Mexico State University.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
